
# derived, rebuildable — never commit
.project/index.db*
STATUS.generated.md
.project/packs/

# kit-entry.sh output. These THREE are derived from the tree and the history: delete them, run
# again, lose nothing. Committing them would put a machine-local snapshot of somebody's working
# copy into every clone, and it would go stale against the tree with nothing detecting it.
.project/entry-facts.tsv
.project/entry-comment-runs.tsv
.project/entry-report.md

# `.project/entry-candidates.md` IS TRACKED, and used to be listed above as a fourth derived
# file. It is not derived. `kit-entry.sh` does not write it -- grep the script -- the model does,
# following docs/ENTRY-PROPOSAL.md, so a re-run cannot reproduce the same judgement the way it
# reproduces a census. It carries open questions, candidate tasks and the evidence for each: a
# PROPOSAL a human reviews, which is the same standing as a task file.
#
# Measured cost of the old classification: a brownfield readiness review concluded "the inventory
# half does not exist in any form" while a complete 89-line proposal sat on disk, because the
# reviewers read HEAD and the file was invisible there. For a trial it is worse than a wrong
# conclusion -- the candidate list IS the deliverable, and an untracked deliverable cannot be
# reported, compared across runs, or handed to the subject's owner.

# Python bytecode. kit_findings.py is imported by tests and by hand; the cache it leaves
# is a build artefact, and one swept in by `git add -A` turned three CI jobs red because the
# exec-bit gate reads every path under tooling/.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
